============================================================
PROJECT NAME
============================================================

ContextPilot

Core idea:

"Give Claude the context it needs — not the context it can find."

ContextPilot is intended to intelligently determine the minimum sufficient code context an AI coding agent needs to perform a software development task.

The problem:

Modern coding agents such as Claude/Copilot can explore repositories themselves, but large enterprise workspaces can contain many repositories, projects, files, dependencies, tests, configurations, shared libraries, etc.

Simply giving the agent access to everything can cause:

- excessive exploration
- irrelevant context
- wasted tokens
- slower reasoning
- repeated repository scanning
- missing important relationships
- poor context prioritization

ContextPilot should help the agent progressively narrow the workspace:

Workspace
    ↓
Relevant repositories
    ↓
Relevant projects
    ↓
Relevant files
    ↓
Relevant symbols
    ↓
Relevant dependencies
    ↓
Relevant tests/configuration
    ↓
Minimum sufficient context


IMPORTANT PRINCIPLE:

"Minimum sufficient context, not maximum available context."

============================================================
ORIGINAL INSPIRATION
============================================================

The project was inspired by Dietrich Gebert's open-source Ponytail Claude Code plugin:

https://github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail

We inspected Ponytail and determined that its main token-reduction mechanism is primarily BEHAVIORAL guidance rather than magical API-level token compression.

Its approach includes a prioritization ladder roughly along the lines of:

1. YAGNI
2. Reuse existing code
3. Standard library
4. Native platform features
5. Existing dependencies
6. One-liners/simple solutions
7. Minimum necessary code

Ponytail also has Claude Code commands/modes such as:

- /ponytail lite
- /ponytail full
- /ponytail ultra
- /ponytail off

and review/audit/debt functionality.

Its repository currently reports benchmark claims around:

- ~54% less code
- ~22% fewer tokens
- ~20% lower cost
- ~27% faster

but these are benchmark claims and the project explicitly distinguishes estimated/measured results.

Important lesson:

ContextPilot should NOT blindly copy Ponytail.

Ponytail mainly influences coding behavior/code minimization.

ContextPilot's focus is:

CONTEXT DISCOVERY + CONTEXT RANKING + CONTEXT OPTIMIZATION.

